WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.042
STEPHANIE WONG: Training
a machine learning model

00:00:02.042 --> 00:00:03.720
involves testing a
variety of parameters

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:05.935
to figure out which
configuration works best.

00:00:05.935 --> 00:00:07.560
Before you even start
training a model,

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.352
you'll need to set up
some hyperparameters,

00:00:09.352 --> 00:00:11.490
like the number of epochs
you want to train for

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.837
or the type of optimization
function you want to use.

00:00:13.837 --> 00:00:16.379
But it can be tricky to figure
out which values will give you

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:17.655
the results you want.

00:00:17.655 --> 00:00:19.530
Let's take a look at
what hyperparameters are

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:21.622
and how Katib can
help you tune them.

00:00:21.622 --> 00:00:24.926
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:28.210
Today we'll talk
about hyperparameters

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.753
and how to use Katib for
hyperparameter tuning.

00:00:30.753 --> 00:00:32.920
If you want to check out
the documentation directly,

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.050
check out the link below
to read more about Katib

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:36.400
and how to use it.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.860
Unlike the training parameters
that are tested and adjusted

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:41.080
during the training
phase, hyperparameters

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:42.520
aren't learned by the model.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:44.650
They're provided before
you begin training and can

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:47.170
make a big impact on the
results of your model.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:48.790
You could try adjusting
them yourself,

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:51.200
changing hyperparameters,
and noting the results.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.170
But when you have many
different configurations

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:54.962
you want to explore
and training your model

00:00:54.962 --> 00:00:56.710
takes a significant
amount of time,

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:58.610
it's not feasible
to do it yourself.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:00.500
That's where Katib
comes in to help.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:02.410
Katib helps tune
your hyperparameters

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:04.599
by testing a range of
values for each one.

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:06.910
You need to define what
your objective is, and then

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:08.410
set up an experiment
so Katib can

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:10.520
try different
hyperparameter values.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.610
For example, if you are
training a model with two

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:15.300
hyperparameters-- learning
rate and batch size--

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.380
and wanted to
maximize the accuracy,

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:19.630
Katib could run the experiment
to try different values

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:21.920
and see how they
affect your objective.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:23.800
You could also try
different objectives,

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.540
like minimizing error rate.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:27.345
Let's walk through
actually running Katib.

00:01:27.345 --> 00:01:28.720
You can try this
out for yourself

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:30.520
if you have a Kubeflow
instance running.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.762
Just head to the dashboard,
choose your namespace,

00:01:32.762 --> 00:01:34.720
and then click Katib on
the left side, followed

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.760
by Hyperparameter Tuning.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:38.500
This should give you
a big text box where

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:40.265
you can paste a YAML file.

00:01:40.265 --> 00:01:41.890
We'll include a link
in the description

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:43.310
for the one we're using here.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:46.630
So go ahead and copy-paste the
file here, and then hit Deploy.

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:48.910
This can take a while to
run, so let's take a look

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:50.840
at some of the configuration
we're specifying.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:54.310
Here we're defining some key
values with our trial accounts.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:57.400
Each trial is an iteration
through the experiment with one

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.530
set of hyperparameter values.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:02.680
This whole file will create
one experiment with 12 trials,

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.840
as specified in
the maxTrialCount.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.810
In addition, Kubeflow will
run three trials in parallel,

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:09.789
thanks to the
parallelTrialCount.

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:12.790
And maxFailedTrialCount will
shut down the experiment

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:15.620
if three of the trials
result in failure.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:18.010
In addition, we're also
specifying the objective

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:18.970
we're looking for.

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:22.570
In this case, we want to
maximize the accuracy_1 metric.

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:25.180
A goal of 0.99 is
also specified,

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:26.680
so the experiment
will automatically

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:29.110
stop if we hit 99% accuracy.

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:31.660
Lastly, we can specify
which algorithm Katib

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.940
will use to tune the
hyperparameter values.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:35.660
Multiple algorithms
are supported,

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.200
such as grid search or
Bayesian optimization,

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:41.200
but we'll keep things simple
with the random algorithm.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.000
In this case,
Katib will randomly

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.310
try different values
for our hyperparameters

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:46.913
and measure our
objective metric.

00:02:46.913 --> 00:02:48.580
Let's take a look at
the hyperparameters

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.110
in the configuration file.

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:51.910
These are the actual
hyperparameters

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:53.030
we want to test.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:55.490
In this case, we're looking
at testing the learning rate

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:57.640
and batch size for
our training step.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:00.310
Each hyperparameter has a
type, along with a feasible

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:02.980
range of values with
minimums and maximums.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.280
Since we specified
random as our algorithm,

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.630
Katib will choose random
values for these two

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:10.330
inside the feasibleSpace and
then run the training job

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:11.770
as a single trial.

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:13.750
Once we've hit
the maxTrialCount,

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:15.415
our experiment
should be finished.

00:03:15.415 --> 00:03:17.290
We can check the status
by selecting the Menu

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:20.930
icon next to Katib and
choosing HP, then Monitor.

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:22.390
You'll see a list
of any experiment

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:23.492
and their current status.

00:03:23.492 --> 00:03:25.450
A check mark means the
experiment has completed

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:28.870
successfully, and we can click
on it to see more information.

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:30.835
This graph can look a
little bit complicated,

00:03:30.835 --> 00:03:32.710
but it's actually just
showing you each trial

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:34.240
over the entire experiment.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.695
The left column is a result
of our objective metric,

00:03:36.695 --> 00:03:38.320
the middle column is
the learning rate,

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.420
and the right column
is our batch size.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.727
If we look at the line that
had the highest accuracy,

00:03:42.727 --> 00:03:44.560
we can see the values
of our hyperparameters

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:45.937
that lead to this run.

00:03:45.937 --> 00:03:48.020
It can be a bit hard to
see the exact values here,

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:49.770
so if you scroll down,
you can see a table

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:50.890
with all the trials.

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:54.340
Looks like the best learning
rate was around 0.0111

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:56.440
and the batch size
was 170, giving us

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:59.230
an accuracy of just over 97%.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:01.940
In this case, we
only used 12 trials,

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:03.550
but we can easily
change the YAML file

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:05.770
and try another experiment
with more trials

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:07.300
or additional hyperparameters.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.610
Katib can help save you
a lot of time and effort

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:11.200
in narrowing down
the best values

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:12.400
for your hyperparameters.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:14.230
And it's easy to set
up an experiment.

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:15.880
Try it out for yourself, and
check out the documentation

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:16.749
and read more.

00:04:16.749 --> 00:04:20.099
[MUSIC PLAYING]

